On Oct 3, 10:02 am, SpotTheDog <rbdav...@gmail.com> wrote:
> One of my pets blundered onto a polymorph trap and changed into a Lich.
> Normally I would bless my scroll of genocide and wipe out all L, but will
> that make my god mad at me if it kills one of my pets?

To the best of my knowledge, killing your pet normally
carries a rather hefty alignment penalty but does not
necessarily cause divine anger (unless you do it in certain
specific ways -- notably, by displacing).

One notable thing about Liches as pets is that they tend
to go feral if you leave their level for a while and come
back.  So you could do that, and then kill it when it's
no longer tame, which would carry no penalty at all.

However, that may not be necessary.  I haven't tested
this, but based on the information in the wiki, it appears
that collateral damage from genocide may not carry the
usual alignment penalty for killing your pet.

Another possibility you could consider, if you have
enough scrolls and/or marker charges, is to uncursed
genocide just the higher and more dangerous grades
of Liches and attempt to keep your pet.  However,
in my experience, keeping a pet in the late game
becomes increasingly difficult as you encounter
larger and larger numbers of levelports, polytraps,
purple worms, black dragons, green slimes, shape
changers, and other pet hazards.  Also, pets are
quite a bit less necessary for your survival in the
late game, compared to the early game, so keeping
your pet beyond about the castle or so may be a
low priority or even actively undesirable, depending
on your play style.


